---
import HubLayout from '../../layouts/HubLayout.astro';
import PullQuote from '../../components/PullQuote.astro';
import MidPageCta from '../../components/MidPageCta.astro';
import { breadcrumbSchema, serviceSchema } from '../../lib/schema';
import RiskWarning from '../../components/RiskWarning.astro';
import TerritorialScope from '../../components/TerritorialScope.astro';

const title = 'Expat Buy-to-Let Mortgage Rates and Criteria';
const description =
  'Buy-to-let mortgages for UK expats: rate ranges, deposit and rental cover requirements, and how lenders treat overseas income.';
const h1 = 'Expat Buy-to-Let Mortgages';
const subheadline =
  'UK buy-to-let mortgages for British nationals living abroad, including building or expanding a portfolio without setting foot in the UK. What lenders look for, how rental income is assessed, what rate ranges to expect, how currency and conveyancing work from overseas, and how a specialist broker finds the right fit.';

const relatedPages = [
  {
    eyebrow: 'Hub',
    title: 'UK Mortgages for Expats',
    description:
      'British nationals living abroad. Expat residential mortgages: deposit, rates, country, and currency.',
    href: '/expat-mortgages',
  },
  {
    eyebrow: 'Hub',
    title: 'UK Mortgages for Non-UK Residents',
    description:
      'Foreign nationals living abroad. Smaller lender pool, larger deposits, and a broker-led process.',
    href: '/non-uk-resident-mortgages',
  },
  {
    eyebrow: 'Hub',
    title: 'UK Mortgages with Foreign Income',
    description:
      'How lenders treat non-sterling income. The 20% haircut and no-haircut lender access.',
    href: '/uk-mortgage-foreign-income',
  },
  {
    eyebrow: 'Guide',
    title: 'Non-Resident Landlord Scheme',
    description:
      'How UK rental income is taxed when you live abroad, and how to register to receive rent without withholding.',
    href: '/guides/non-resident-landlord-scheme',
  },
  {
    eyebrow: 'Guide',
    title: 'Non-UK Resident Stamp Duty',
    description:
      'The 2% SDLT surcharge, how it stacks with the additional dwelling surcharge, and when a rebate is available.',
    href: '/guides/stamp-duty-non-uk-resident',
  },
  {
    eyebrow: 'Guide',
    title: 'Buying UK Property from Overseas',
    description:
      'The full purchase process from finding a property to completion when you live abroad.',
    href: '/guides/buying-uk-property-from-overseas',
  },
  {
    eyebrow: 'Guide',
    title: 'UK Rental Market and Landlord Statistics 2026',
    description:
      'Sourced ONS, HomeLet, UK Finance and government landlord survey statistics on UK rents, buy-to-let lending, and landlord portfolio structure.',
    href: '/guides/uk-rental-market-statistics',
  },
  {
    eyebrow: 'Guide',
    title: 'Holiday Let Mortgages',
    description:
      'How holiday let mortgages differ from standard buy-to-let, income assessment, and the tax position since April 2025.',
    href: '/guides/holiday-let-mortgages',
  },
  {
    eyebrow: 'Guide',
    title: 'Let to Buy Mortgages',
    description:
      'How a let to buy mortgage works when you keep your current UK property as a rental while buying your next UK home.',
    href: '/guides/let-to-buy-mortgages',
  },
];

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What rates can I expect on an expat BTL mortgage?',
    a: 'Expat BTL rates are typically 0.4% to 1.0% above equivalent UK-resident BTL products at the same loan-to-value. As of 2026, expat BTL fixed rates typically sit in the upper-4s to mid-5s percent range, depending on deposit, lender, and term, although rates change frequently.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What deposit do I need for an expat BTL?',
    a: 'Usually 25% to 35%. The exact figure depends on the lender and the property. Some lenders accept 25% on standard properties for stronger applicants. Properties with higher perceived risk (HMOs, multi-units, certain new builds) may require 35% or more.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I use foreign income to support an expat BTL application?',
    a: 'Yes, but BTL borrowing is primarily based on rental income through ICR, not personal income. Some lenders apply a minimum personal income requirement (often £25,000 to £40,000 equivalent) to qualify for expat BTL at all, but the personal income is a hurdle to clear rather than a multiplier on borrowing.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I switch a UK property from residential to BTL when I move abroad?',
    a: 'Yes. The first step is usually consent to let, which is temporary permission from your residential lender to rent the property. Most lenders grant this for 12 to 24 months. After that period, or if the lender will not extend, you remortgage to a buy-to-let product. An expat BTL remortgage is the standard long-term solution.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Are stamp duty rules different for expat BTL?',
    a: 'Yes. Expat BTL purchases attract standard SDLT rates, the 5% additional dwelling surcharge (for second properties or BTL), and the 2% non-resident surcharge (because you have been outside the UK for the 12 months before completion). The combined surcharges are meaningful.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I buy-to-let through a limited company as an expat?',
    a: 'Yes. The lender pool for limited company expat BTL is smaller than for personal name (perhaps 8 to 10 lenders) but well established. Most limited company expat BTL is done through SPVs set up specifically for property holding.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the minimum loan size for expat BTL?',
    a: 'Usually £100,000 to £150,000 at most lenders. A handful of specialists go lower, but the trend is upward.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I get a 75% LTV expat BTL?',
    a: 'Yes, often. 75% LTV is the standard maximum for expat BTL across most of the lender pool. A few lenders go to 80% for specific applicant profiles. Higher LTVs come with higher rate premiums.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I need to come to the UK to complete the purchase?',
    a: 'No. You sign the completion documents with a notary public in your country of residence, who certifies them for use in the UK. Some embassies and consulates also handle this. Budget two to four weeks for round-trip notarisation.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I manage currency risk on my deposit?',
    a: 'Most expat deposits start in a foreign currency and need converting to sterling before completion. A forward contract with an FX broker locks the rate ahead of completion and removes timing risk. Converting at exchange or in tranches are the other common approaches.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I remortgage an expat BTL from abroad?',
    a: 'Yes. This is common as fixed terms end. The process runs the same way as the original application: broker-led, with documents signed and certified from wherever you live.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does a bigger deposit mean a better rate?',
    a: 'Yes, materially. The step from 25% to 30% is the biggest single lever on price, and 35% or more unlocks a further improvement at most lenders.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Are five-year fixes worth the small premium?',
    a: 'For most expat BTL landlords, yes. The five-year fix usually costs a little more upfront and removes the remortgage exercise at the two-year mark, which takes longer from abroad.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does my country of residence affect the rate?',
    a: 'Indirectly, through the currency you are paid in. The currency on your payslip moves the rate more than the country on your address.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Are limited company BTL rates higher?',
    a: 'Marginally, and lender fees can be higher. For higher-rate taxpayers the tax treatment usually outweighs the rate difference. The bigger effect is a narrower lender pool.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Are rates worse if I am self-employed?',
    a: 'Slightly, with a smaller pool of lenders willing to look at the case. Two years of accounts and a clean documentation pack narrow the gap.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I lock the rate in advance of completion?',
    a: 'Yes. Most lenders hold the rate for three to six months from offer, which usually covers a purchase running to a normal timetable.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can foreign nationals access similar BTL rates to expats?',
    a: 'Rate ranges are broadly similar. The criteria are stricter for foreign nationals, so the constraint is which lenders will accept the case rather than what they charge.',
  },
];

const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: f.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
  })),
};
const schemas = [
  breadcrumbSchema([
    { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
    { name: 'UK Mortgages for Expats', path: '/expat-mortgages' },
    { name: 'Expat Buy-to-Let', path: '/expat-mortgages/buy-to-let' },
  ]),
  serviceSchema({ serviceType: 'UK Expat Buy-to-Let Mortgage Information', name: h1, description }),
];
---

<HubLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  h1={h1}
  subheadline={subheadline}
  eyebrow="Hub · Expat BTL"
  ctaHeading="Speak to a specialist mortgage broker"
  schemas={schemas}
  relatedPages={relatedPages}
>
  <RiskWarning type="btl" />
  <section class="hub-section">
    <div class="hub-section-inner">
      <p
        class="subheadline"
        style="max-width: 680px; margin: 0 auto 1.5rem; text-align: center;"
      >
        UK expat buy-to-let mortgages are available from around 12 to 15
        active lenders, usually at a 25% to 35% deposit and rates 0.4 to 1.0
        percentage points above equivalent UK-resident buy-to-let.
        Applications, conveyancing and completion are all handled from
        abroad, with no need to return to the UK.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <TerritorialScope />
  <section class="hub-section">
    <div class="hub-section-inner">
      <h2 id="who-this-is-for">Who this page is for</h2>
      <p>
        If you are a UK national living abroad and you want to buy a UK
        property as a rental investment, this page is for you. Common
        scenarios:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          A UK national working abroad on a multi-year posting, building a
          UK property portfolio for return.
        </li>
        <li>
          A UK national permanently abroad, holding UK property as a
          sterling-denominated investment.
        </li>
        <li>
          A UK national already with a UK BTL portfolio, expanding it from
          abroad.
        </li>
        <li>
          A UK national wanting to keep their previous UK home as a rental
          after moving abroad. If you are in this position, start with our
          <a href="/guides/consent-to-let">consent to let guide</a>, which
          explains the first step before a BTL remortgage becomes necessary.
        </li>
        <li>
          A UK national restructuring an existing portfolio into a limited
          company while living abroad.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        None of this requires a trip back to the UK. Applications,
        conveyancing, and completion are all routinely handled while you
        stay wherever you live.
      </p>
      <p>
        The lender pool, criteria, and process are similar to expat
        residential mortgages with a few BTL-specific differences. This
        page covers those differences.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you are a foreign national rather than a UK expat, see our
        <a href="/non-uk-resident-mortgages/buy-to-let">non-UK resident buy-to-let</a>
        page instead.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="hub-section">
    <div class="hub-section-inner">
      <h2 id="why-it-works">Why build a UK portfolio from abroad</h2>
      <p>
        UK buy-to-let from overseas has been a durable strategy for British
        expats for two reasons in particular. It gives you a
        sterling-denominated asset and income stream in a market you
        already understand, rather than local property in a jurisdiction
        with unfamiliar rules. And it builds income and a base for an
        eventual return to the UK, or for family who study or work here in
        the meantime.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <MidPageCta variant="hero" />

  <section class="hub-section">
    <div class="hub-section-inner">
      <h2 id="can-expat-get-btl">Can an expat get a UK buy-to-let mortgage?</h2>
      <p>
        Yes. UK BTL lending to expats is an active market with around 12 to
        15 lenders working in the space regularly. The lender pool is
        slightly smaller than for expat residential, but the lenders that
        do operate here are well-established in this niche.
      </p>
      <p>Whether a particular lender will lend to you depends on:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Where you live now.</li>
        <li>Your currency of income.</li>
        <li>The property type and location in the UK.</li>
        <li>Your existing UK property portfolio (if any).</li>
        <li>Your overall financial profile.</li>
        <li>Whether you are buying personally or through a limited company.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Most expat BTL applicants pass these checks. Cases that get more
        complex involve unusual countries of residence, large or growing
        portfolios, properties of unusual type (HMOs, multi-units, certain
        commercial-on-residential conversions), or limited company
        structures with multiple directors.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="hub-section">
    <div class="hub-section-inner">
      <h2 id="whats-different">What is different from a standard UK BTL mortgage</h2>
      <p>Four things are usually different.</p>
      <p>
        <strong><a href="/expat-mortgages/deposit">Deposit is higher.</a></strong> Expat BTL typically requires
        25% to 35% deposit. UK-resident BTL can be done with 20% to 25%.
        Some lenders require 40% on stronger LTVs or for certain property
        types.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Rates are slightly higher.</strong> <a href="#btl-rates">Expat BTL rates</a> are
        usually 0.4% to 1.0% above equivalent UK-resident BTL products at
        the same loan-to-value. The gap narrows for larger deposits.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>ICR is stricter.</strong> UK BTL lenders calculate
        borrowing using an interest cover ratio (ICR), which is the rental
        income divided by the mortgage interest at a stressed rate. For UK
        residents, ICR is typically 125% to 145%. For expats, ICR is
        usually 145% to 160%, depending on lender and tax band. This is
        the single biggest mechanical difference and the one that tends to
        constrain expat BTL borrowing power. Where a lender also sets a
        minimum personal income, your overseas salary is converted and
        haircut in the same way as on a residential case. Use the
        <a href="/tools/expat-borrowing-power">expat borrowing power tracker</a>
        to see what your salary supports at today's exchange rate.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Lender pool is smaller.</strong> Around 12 to 15 lenders
        operate here, against perhaps 50 for UK-resident BTL.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <PullQuote>
    Around 12 to 15 lenders operate here, against perhaps 50 for
    UK-resident BTL.
  </PullQuote>

  <section class="hub-section">
    <div class="hub-section-inner">
      <h2 id="how-icr-works">How ICR works for expat BTL</h2>
      <p>
        ICR is the maths the lender does to decide how much to lend. It is
        rental income divided by mortgage interest at a stressed rate,
        expressed as a percentage.
      </p>
      <p>A worked example for an expat applicant in the higher-rate tax band:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Property monthly rent: £1,800</li>
        <li>Lender stressed rate (ICR test rate): 7%</li>
        <li>ICR requirement: 145%</li>
        <li>
          Monthly mortgage interest must not exceed: £1,800 / 1.45 = £1,241
        </li>
        <li>Loan supportable: roughly £213,000 at 7% interest-only</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        So a £1,800 monthly rent supports a loan of around £213,000 at this
        ICR. If the property costs £300,000, the deposit is £87,000 (29%).
        If the property costs £350,000, the deposit needs to be £137,000
        (39%) to fit the same rental income.
      </p>
      <p>
        A different lender might use 145% ICR at a lower stressed rate,
        supporting a higher loan on the same rent. Another might apply
        160% ICR for a higher-rate taxpayer, lowering supportable loan.
        The variation between lenders is meaningful and is the reason a
        <a href="/expat-mortgage-broker">specialist broker</a> matters more in BTL than in residential.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <PullQuote>
    The variation between lenders is meaningful and is the reason a
    specialist broker matters more in BTL than in residential.
  </PullQuote>

  <section class="hub-section">
    <div class="hub-section-inner">
      <h2 id="personal-vs-ltd">Personal name vs limited company</h2>
      <p>
        Expat BTL can be done in your personal name or through a limited
        company (often a special-purpose vehicle, or SPV, set up
        specifically for property holding).
      </p>

      <h3>Personal name</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Lower fees on the mortgage.</li>
        <li>Simpler tax structure for smaller portfolios.</li>
        <li>
          Mortgage interest tax relief restricted under Section 24 rules,
          which affects higher-rate taxpayers significantly.
        </li>
        <li>
          Fewer lenders in the expat space lend to limited companies than
          to personal-name applicants, but the gap is closing.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Limited company (SPV)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Mortgage interest is fully deductible against rental income
          before tax (no Section 24 restriction).
        </li>
        <li>
          Lower effective tax rate for higher-rate UK taxpayers in many
          cases.
        </li>
        <li>Higher arrangement fees and legal costs.</li>
        <li>
          Slightly smaller lender pool than personal name in the expat
          space.
        </li>
        <li>
          More common for expats building a portfolio than for expats
          holding one or two properties.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        The right structure depends on tax position, residence, and
        portfolio plans. This is a tax decision more than a mortgage
        decision and worth discussing with an accountant before
        committing.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you will be letting the property while living abroad, register
        with the <a href="/guides/non-resident-landlord-scheme">Non-Resident
        Landlord Scheme</a> before rent starts. Without registration, your
        letting agent is required to withhold 20% basic-rate tax from every
        rent payment before passing it to you. The
        <a href="/guides/nrl1-form-explained">NRL1 form</a> is how you
        register.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="hub-section">
    <div class="hub-section-inner">
      <h2 id="currency-timing">Currency timing on your deposit</h2>
      <p>
        A practical issue specific to buying from abroad. Most expat
        deposits sit in a foreign currency, not sterling. Converting to
        pounds at the wrong moment can move the effective deposit
        materially: a 10% adverse currency move on an £80,000 deposit is
        £8,000 of unintended cost.
      </p>
      <p>Three common approaches:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Convert at exchange.</strong> Lock the rate the day you
          commit to the purchase. Simple, but you take whatever the rate
          is that day.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Forward contract.</strong> An FX broker locks a rate for
          delivery at completion. Common for larger transactions and the
          cleanest way to remove timing risk, for a small spread.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Convert in tranches.</strong> Spread the conversion over
          the weeks or months ahead of the expected purchase, smoothing the
          rate.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        For most BTL purchases, a forward contract via a reputable FX
        broker is worth setting up before you start seriously looking at
        property.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="hub-section">
    <div class="hub-section-inner">
      <h2 id="conveyancing">Conveyancing and signing from abroad</h2>
      <p>
        The legal side of a UK purchase happens in the UK regardless of
        where you live, which raises a few practical points from overseas.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Solicitor selection.</strong> Use a UK conveyancer used to
        working with non-resident clients. Most established firms are.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Signing documents.</strong> You sign with a notary public
        in your country of residence, who certifies the document for use
        in the UK. Some embassies and consulates also handle this. Budget
        two to four weeks for round-trip notarisation.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Time zones.</strong> UK office hours rarely match Asia or
        the US West Coast. Email and WhatsApp tend to work better than
        scheduled calls.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Anti-money-laundering checks.</strong> Standard UK
        conveyancing AML applies: source of funds, identity verification,
        and PEP checks. From abroad, this typically needs more supporting
        documentation than from a UK-resident position, so it is worth
        starting early. Our <a href="/guides/buying-uk-property-from-overseas">guide to buying UK property from overseas</a>
        covers the wider purchase timeline in full.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="hub-section">
    <div class="hub-section-inner">
      <h2 id="what-you-need">What you will usually need</h2>
      <p>
        The exact requirements vary by lender. The list below covers what
        most expat BTL lenders will ask for.
      </p>

      <h3>Identity and residency</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>UK passport.</li>
        <li>Proof of current address abroad.</li>
        <li>Visa or residency permit, where applicable.</li>
        <li>Tax residency confirmation.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Income evidence</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Three to twelve months of payslips.</li>
        <li>Two years of P60s or country-equivalent tax returns.</li>
        <li>An employer reference letter in many cases.</li>
        <li>
          For <a href="/expat-mortgages/self-employed">self-employed applicants</a>: two to three years of accounts plus
          accountant reference.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Existing portfolio (if any)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Schedule of existing UK rental properties showing addresses,
          mortgage balances, monthly rents, lender names, and current
          values.
        </li>
        <li>Bank statements showing rental income deposits.</li>
        <li>
          For portfolio landlords (4+ mortgaged properties), a portfolio
          stress test will be run.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Property and deposit</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Memorandum of sale.</li>
        <li>
          Independent rental valuation (the lender will instruct this as
          part of the survey).
        </li>
        <li>Deposit source documentation.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Limited company applications also need</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          SPV company accounts (if existing) or details of newly formed
          SPV.
        </li>
        <li>
          Director personal financial details for the personal guarantee
          that lenders typically require.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="hub-section">
    <div class="hub-section-inner">
      <h2 id="portfolio">Portfolio landlord rules</h2>
      <p>
        If you have four or more mortgaged BTL properties, lenders treat
        you as a portfolio landlord. The application is more involved.
      </p>
      <p>What this means in practice:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          The lender stress-tests the entire portfolio, not just the
          property being bought or remortgaged.
        </li>
        <li>
          A portfolio spreadsheet covering all properties, mortgages,
          rents, and values is required.
        </li>
        <li>
          The portfolio overall typically needs to meet a minimum ICR
          (125% to 135% across all properties).
        </li>
        <li>
          Some lenders cap the percentage of the portfolio that can be at
          high LTV.
        </li>
        <li>
          Some lenders do not lend to portfolio landlords at all. The
          lender pool narrows further.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Portfolio cases benefit particularly from broker involvement.
        Different lenders apply portfolio rules differently, and the right
        placement can be the difference between an offer and a decline.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="hub-section">
    <div class="hub-section-inner">
      <h2 id="how-the-process-works">How the application process works</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Step 1: Initial conversation.</strong> You speak with a
        broker about the property, your existing portfolio (if any), your
        income, your country of residence, and your structure preference
        (personal name or limited company). 30 to 45 minutes.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Step 2: Decision in principle.</strong> The broker
        approaches a lender for a DIP. Returned within 24 to 72 hours.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Step 3: Documentation gathering.</strong> The broker tells
        you exactly what to pull together. For expat BTL this often
        includes portfolio spreadsheets, accountant references, and
        certified copies of foreign documents. A few weeks typically.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Step 4: Full application.</strong> Submitted to the
        lender. Survey instructed.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Step 5: Underwriting.</strong> Two to six weeks for expat
        BTL cases.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Step 6: Mortgage offer.</strong> Valid three to six months.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Step 7: Completion.</strong> Solicitor handles legal
        exchange and completion.
      </p>
      <p>
        A typical timeline is 10 to 14 weeks. Portfolio cases often run
        longer because of the portfolio stress test.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="hub-section">
    <div class="hub-section-inner">
      <h2 id="why-broker-matters">Why a specialist broker matters in BTL</h2>
      <p>
        Expat residential and expat BTL are both broker-friendly markets.
        BTL is more so. Three reasons.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>ICR variation between lenders is large.</strong> Different
        lenders apply different stress rates and different ICR
        percentages. The same property at the same rent supports very
        different loan sizes depending on which lender is approached. A
        broker who knows the current ICR map can usually find the lender
        that supports the loan you need.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Portfolio rules are not standardised.</strong> Some lenders
        only count mortgaged properties in your portfolio. Some count all
        properties including unencumbered. Some apply different stress
        tests to background portfolio than to the new application. A
        broker placing portfolio cases regularly knows which lenders work
        for your specific shape.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Some of the strongest expat BTL lenders are
        broker-only.</strong> Several specialist building societies and
        private banks do not market to retail customers. Direct
        application is not possible. A broker is the only route.
      </p>
      <p>
        We place expat BTL cases regularly and have direct access to
        several specialist lenders that other brokers do not.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="hub-section">
    <div class="hub-section-inner">
      <h2 id="btl-rates">Expat buy-to-let mortgage rates</h2>
      <p>
        Expat buy-to-let prices above both of its neighbours. Roughly 0.5
        to 1 percentage point above expat residential borrowing, and 0.4%
        to 1.0% above the equivalent UK-resident BTL product at the same
        loan-to-value. Three things create that premium.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Investment lending carries more credit risk.</strong> A
        landlord under financial pressure protects their own home before
        the rental property. Lenders price for that.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>The stress test sets a floor.</strong> UK-resident BTL is
        commonly tested at 125% ICR on a notional 5.5% rate. Expat BTL is
        tested at 145% or higher on 6% or more, as the ICR section above
        sets out. Lenders then set product rates so most applications can
        still clear the harder test.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Competition is thinner.</strong> Only around a fifth of UK
        BTL lenders accept non-resident applicants, and inside that group
        there is less pressure on price than in the open UK-resident
        market.
      </p>

      <h3>The current range</h3>
      <p>
        The band is wide because the lender pool is mixed. Mainstream
        lenders that accept expat BTL price keenly for clean profiles.
        Specialist expat lenders price for the extra underwriting work.
        Private banks price larger loans on their own terms and can beat
        both. Through 2026 that has generally meant fixed rates in the
        upper 4s to mid 5s, although pricing moves frequently. A clean
        profile with a 30% deposit, income in a major currency and a
        property in an established rental market sits in the middle of the
        band. Smaller deposits, minor currencies and unusual property
        types push up. Larger deposits and high-net-worth positions pull
        down. For the residential comparison, see our
        <a href="/expat-mortgages/rates">expat mortgage rates</a> guide.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two-year and five-year fixes dominate. The five-year fix usually
        costs a little more upfront and removes the remortgage exercise at
        the two-year mark, which is worth more to an expat than to a UK
        resident because remortgaging from abroad takes longer.
      </p>

      <h3>What moves the rate you are offered</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Lender choice</strong> is the largest single variable. The
        same case put to two lenders can come back 0.3 to 0.5 percentage
        points apart. On a £300,000 loan over a five-year fix, that is
        £4,500 to £7,500 of interest.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Deposit</strong> is next. The step from 25% to 30% is the
        biggest lever, with the following meaningful threshold at 40%.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Currency</strong> matters more than the country you live
        in. USD, EUR and CHF are accepted everywhere. The dollar-pegged
        Gulf currencies and the Hong Kong dollar are widely accepted and
        lightly priced. SGD, AUD and NZD price a little heavier. ZAR and
        the minor currencies face the narrowest choice and the toughest
        pricing.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Rental yield</strong> decides whether the loan you want
        clears ICR at all. A property yielding 6% gross clears
        comfortably. At 4%, the options are a smaller loan, a lower-stress
        lender, or more deposit.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Property type</strong> sorts the pool. Standard flats and
        houses in established markets price well. New-build, ex-council,
        HMOs and <a href="/guides/holiday-let-mortgages">holiday lets</a>
        attract restrictions or a premium.
      </p>

      <h3>Where different profiles land</h3>
      <p>
        Working ranges rather than guarantees. A British expat in the UAE
        earning dirhams with a major employer and a 30% deposit on a
        London rental sits toward the bottom of the expat band, close to
        mainstream specialist pricing. A US-based expat on salary plus
        bonus and equity with 25% down sits mid-band, with room to improve
        by moving to a lender that counts vested equity. A Hong Kong
        banking package with 35% down sits mid to low, and often attracts
        private bank interest on the larger loans. A South Africa-based
        expat earning rand sits at the top of the band, because few
        lenders take the currency. A first-time landlord buying from
        abroad sits mid to high, and first-time landlord status alone
        rules some lenders out entirely.
      </p>
      <p>
        An established expat landlord adding to a portfolio usually prices
        better than a first-timer. A remortgage at fix end prices on the
        current market plus your payment record on the existing loan, and
        is worth starting four to six months out. Holding through a
        limited company barely moves the rate but narrows the lender pool,
        and lender fees can run higher.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two things you control compress the gap. The deposit you put down,
        and a documentation pack that is complete before the application
        goes in. A lender that has to chase paperwork underwrites
        cautiously, and caution shows up in the price.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

